-#define SCREAMER_PREROLL_MS 30
+/*
+ * RetroBoot: the codec's clock runs in real time, whatever QEMU's or the
+ * guest's latency (package r12 let it slip).
+ *
+ * Each output command used to complete one play time after the moment it
+ * started, so every delay before the next command started was lost for
+ * good: the main loop's timer latency (some 15 us per 1 ms command on an
+ * idle host, more under load), and above all the time Mac OS takes to
+ * re-arm its DBDMA program when its sound interrupt is serviced late (on a
+ * slow host the program ran out every 70 ms and sat 10-23 ms until Mac OS
+ * restarted it).  The DMA delivered 4.6 s of a QuickTime soundtrack in 5.1 s
+ * on the lab builder and in 9.4 s with its vCPU at 30 % of a core, so the
+ * host stream, consumed in real time, ran dry over and over (ALSA
+ * underruns on the N5105 and N4100 laptops: sound in bursts).
+ *
+ * Now the codec keeps a continuous timeline: a command completes when the
+ * codec, counting from where the previous one ended, has played it, less
+ * dma-lead-us (the DMA engine runs that far ahead of the codec, like the
+ * hardware's FIFO).  Behind that timeline the DMA catches up at up to
+ * twice real time, spaced on its own schedule (timer latency does not slow
+ * it) or, after it sat idle, from the moment it starts again; twice real
+ * time keeps the guest's half-buffer interrupts apart (all at once, they
+ * merge: package r11).  A pause longer than resync-ms ends the stream: the
+ * next sound starts a new timeline.
+ *
+ * The host stream starts once the current lead (host-lead-ms at first) is
+ * queued.  If it then runs dry while the DMA is running (QEMU handed the
+ * host less than it has played since the stream started) the lead grows by
+ * SCREAMER_LEAD_STEP_MS up to host-lead-max-ms and the stream pre-rolls
+ * again; the lead shrinks one step per SCREAMER_LEAD_DECAY_S without an
+ * underrun, when a stream starts.  When the host falls a whole buffer
+ * (371 ms) behind, the oldest audio is dropped: the guest keeps its
+ * real-time pace (QuickTime's clock runs from it) instead of waiting.
+ */
+#define SCREAMER_LEAD_STEP_MS   20
+#define SCREAMER_IDLE_NS        (2 * SCALE_MS)  /* longer: the DMA was idle */
+#define SCREAMER_LEAD_DECAY_S   20
 
 static void screamer_update_volume(ScreamerState *s);
 
